ARTICLE 13, AND WHERE IT WOULD BITE
-----------------------------------

Razar ships as a command-line binary. It serves nothing over a network, so
section 13 of the AGPL does not come into play as the project stands.

It would come into play for anyone who puts the organism behind a network
interface — a web front end, an API, an internal service reachable by others.
In that case the AGPL requires that whoever interacts with it over the network
can obtain the corresponding source of the whole service, not only of Razar.
That is the specific difference between the AGPL and the GPL, and it is the
reason this license was chosen: an organism is exactly the kind of thing
somebody would serve rather than distribute.


